description:
Build a small version of what the MSVC debug heap does (lesson 4.03): fill new memory with a pattern, surround every block with guard bytes, check them on free, keep freed blocks aside to catch writes after free, catch double frees and frees of unknown pointers, and report leaks.

```cpp
enum class Problem { InvalidFree, DoubleFree, Underrun, Overrun, WriteAfterFree };
struct Report { Problem problem; int allocation; };

class DebugHeap
{
public:
    static constexpr std::uint8_t CLEAN{ 0xCD }, DEAD{ 0xDD }, GUARD{ 0xFD };
    static constexpr std::size_t GUARD_BYTES{ 4 };

    ~DebugHeap();
    void* allocate(std::size_t size);
    void release(void* p);
    void checkFreed();
    std::vector<int> leaks() const;
    const std::vector<Report>& problems() const;
    std::size_t bytesInUse() const;
};
```

- **allocate** gets `GUARD_BYTES + size + GUARD_BYTES` bytes from `std::malloc`, fills the guards with `GUARD` and the user area with `CLEAN`, and returns a pointer to the user area. Allocations are numbered 1, 2, 3, ... in order (0 bytes is allowed and still gets a number).
- **release**:
  - a pointer this heap never returned: report `InvalidFree` with allocation 0;
  - a block already released: report `DoubleFree` with its number;
  - otherwise check the guard before the block (`Underrun` if any byte changed) and after it (`Overrun`), fill the user area with `DEAD`, and keep the block in **quarantine** instead of freeing it.
- **checkFreed** looks at every quarantined block: if any user byte is no longer `DEAD`, report `WriteAfterFree` (once per block per check).
- **leaks** lists the numbers of blocks never released, in increasing order; **bytesInUse** adds up their sizes.
- **problems** returns every report so far, in the order they happened.
- The destructor really frees every block, quarantined or not.

The tests deliberately write just outside blocks and into freed blocks. That is safe here only because the guard bytes and the quarantine are your own memory.

